"""Assembly sequence for the selected kit; physical completion is user-recorded."""

def render():
    def picture(name, alt, caption):
        return f'<figure class="blender-view"><a href="assets/blender/{name}.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="assets/blender/{name}.png" alt="{alt}"></a><figcaption>{caption}</figcaption></figure>'
    def step(n, title, parts, actions, check):
        return f'<article class="assembly-step" id="step-{n}"><span class="badge">Step {n:02}</span><h3>{title}</h3><p class="small"><strong>On the bench:</strong> {parts}</p><ol>'+''.join(f'<li>{a}</li>' for a in actions)+f'</ol><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="assembly-v1-{n}">{check}</label></article>'
    body='''<div class="callout"><strong>Build the robot first, then add one light tool.</strong> For the current paper-grown microgreens, our recommendation is to defer the moisture probe, probe relay and contact holder. Existing cameras plus human water checks support the first supervised trial. This does not yet provide unattended watering.</div>
<div class="toc"><a href="#kit">Identify your kit</a><a href="#inventory">Kit inventory</a><a href="#mechanical">1–4 · Mechanical</a><a href="#computer">5–8 · Mac and motion</a><a href="#sensors">9–11 · Light tool</a><a href="#watering">12–14 · Trays and water</a><a href="#later">15–16 · Optional parts</a><a href="#connections">Connection checklist</a></div>
<p>Tick each checkpoint only after doing it. Checks save in this browser, using the same notebook as the growing trial. This page cannot sense the robot or run it.</p>
<section id="kit"><h2>Use the instructions for the two-wheel kit</h2><p>Your transcript records the WowRobo Combo with assembled SO101 follower arms, two directional drive wheels, two head servos, three cameras, motor boards, robot wiring and two 12V supplies. The current vendor listing calls this XLeRobot 0.4.0. Check the packing list and labels before assembly; the delivered revision has not been inspected here.</p><div class="callout warn"><strong>Use the 0.4.0 video, not the 0.3.0 one.</strong> The two-wheel kit is XLeRobot <strong>0.4.0</strong>; its official build video is <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4bXCFw57T60">“XLeRobot 0.4.0 Assembly Guide” (WowRobo)</a>. The older <a href="https://youtu.be/upB1CEFeOlk">0.3.0 video</a> — which the WowRobo listing still links — shows the three-omni-wheel base and a different top plate and head; follow it only for the arms if needed. Check your box first: two large drive wheels plus casters = 0.4.0; three omni wheels = 0.3.0.</div><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4bXCFw57T60" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch the 0.4.0 (two-wheel) assembly video</a><a class="button secondary" href="https://xlerobot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/hardware/getting_started/assemble_2wheel.html">Two-wheel reference</a><a class="button secondary" href="https://youtu.be/upB1CEFeOlk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0.3.0 video (three-wheel, arms only)</a></div><p>The dedicated two-wheel documentation is still under construction. Use the vendor video for the exact order of brackets, bearings and screws. The older general guide depicts three omni-wheels: its wheel positions, third motor and motor-ID map must not be copied into this build. The steps below organize our integration work; they do not invent missing vendor dimensions or screw lengths. <a href="https://shop.wowrobo.com/products/xlerobot-dual-arm-mobile-household-robot-kit">Vendor listing</a>.</p></section>
<section id="inventory"><h2>What is in the WowRobo Combo box (XLeRobot 0.4.0, two-wheel)</h2><p>From the vendor listings for “Package 3: Combo – Dual-Arm + Base Expansion”. Tick each line against your actual box; the vendor does not publish screw counts, so the fastener bags are checked as groups.</p><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Qty</th><th>What it is for</th><th>Arrives</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>SO-ARM101 follower arm</td><td>2</td><td>Left and right arm. Each has 6 STS3215 servos (IDs 1–6), a motor control board on its base and a gripper.</td><td>Assembled</td></tr>
<tr><td>Motor control board</td><td>2 (one per arm base)</td><td>USB-C to the Mac (data), barrel jack for 12 V. Board 1 drives left arm + head; board 2 drives right arm + wheels.</td><td>On the arms</td></tr>
<tr><td>2 MP USB camera</td><td>3</td><td>Two wrist cameras (one per arm) and one head camera.</td><td>Wrist cams may be fitted; head cam loose</td></tr>
<tr><td>STS3215 servo, 12 V</td><td>4 loose</td><td>Head pan (ID 7), head tilt (ID 8), left wheel (ID 9), right wheel (ID 10).</td><td>Loose; IDs must be set</td></tr>
<tr><td>Directional drive wheel</td><td>2</td><td>The two large wheels of the 0.4.0 base.</td><td>Loose</td></tr>
<tr><td>12 V / 8 A power supply</td><td>2</td><td>One per motor control board. Check the label reads 100–240 V for Japan.</td><td>Boxed</td></tr>
<tr><td>PLA structural parts</td><td>1 set</td><td>Drive-motor mounts, base plates and cart connectors, top plate / arm bases, head pan-tilt parts, camera mounts.</td><td>Printed, unassembled</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bearings, screws, screw seats, fasteners</td><td>bags</td><td>Wheel bearings (30 × 37 × 4 mm), M3/M4 screws, heat-set seats.</td><td>Bagged</td></tr>
<tr><td>Servo cables + long extension leads</td><td>set</td><td>3-pin servo daisy-chain leads; long leads from the wheels up to the right arm and from the head to the left arm.</td><td>Bagged</td></tr>
<tr><td>USB data cables for the boards</td><td>2</td><td>Board → hub.</td><td>Bagged</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Not in the box (you have these):</strong> IKEA RÅSKOG cart, RSHTECH 7-port powered hub, MacBook, Anker C300 (unused in V0). <strong>Not in the box and not needed:</strong> leader arms, Raspberry Pi, depth camera, game controller. <strong>Tools:</strong> small Phillips and hex drivers (the kit’s fasteners decide sizes), flush cutter for the cart mesh, a marker. Sources: <a href="https://openelab.io/products/wowrobo-robotics-xlerobot-dual-arm">OpenELAB listing (itemised)</a>, <a href="https://shop.wowrobo.com/products/xlerobot-dual-arm-mobile-household-robot-kit">WowRobo listing</a>, <a href="https://xlerobot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/hardware/getting_started/assemble_2wheel.html">XLeRobot dual-wheel page</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>Order of work for this kit:</strong> (1) inventory → (2) set IDs 7, 8, 9, 10 on the four loose servos with <code>farm set-motor-id</code> (<a href="guide.html#step-motor-ids">guide step</a>; browser fallback: <a href="https://bambot.org/feetech.js">bambot.org/feetech.js</a> in Chrome) → (3) build the cart → (4) wheel servos into the drive mounts, base under the cart → (5) top plate, arms, head → (6) wiring: board 1 = left arm → head 7 → 8; board 2 = right arm → wheels 9 → 10 → (7) USB to the hub, 12 V last → (8) <a href="guide.html#p4">software bring-up</a>. Once built, lift the robot to move it; pushing it back-drives the wheel gears.</div></section>
<section id="mechanical"><h2>1–4 · Build a stable, dry robot</h2>'''
    body+=step(1,'Lay out and identify the parts','WowRobo boxes, IKEA cart, hub, Mac; leave the battery disconnected.',[
        'Group the two arms and their motor controllers, two drive assemblies, head assembly, three cameras, bracket/fastener bags, two motor supplies and supplied USB/motor leads. Keep the original bags with their labels.',
        'Photograph motor and adapter labels. Confirm this is the two-wheel servo version and that each motor supply matches its controller and motors. An adapter fitting the socket is not sufficient.',
        'Set aside the farm sensors, growing parts and battery. Have a screwdriver/hex keys matching the supplied screws and a borrowed multimeter for electrical checks.'], 'Kit revision, voltage labels and all supplied part groups identified.')
    body+=step(2,'Assemble the IKEA cart and drive base','Cart, two drive modules, printed brackets, bearings, supplied screws and motor extensions.',[
        'Assemble the cart to its own manual. Before adding the arms, compare the bracket fit with the two-wheel vendor video; do not force a bracket designed for a different cart size.',
        'Build and mount each drive module in the video’s orientation and screw order. Use the kit’s bearings, wheel hubs and fasteners; do not substitute the older three-wheel plate.',
        'Route supplied motor extensions upward with protected edges and slack. Keep wheels free of dangling leads. Do not cut cart mesh until the delivered bracket and cable route establish where an opening is needed.'], 'Base sits securely, wheels clear the structure, and no lead can enter a wheel.')
    body+=step(3,'Mount the arms and head','Assembled follower arms, top mounts/clamps, head pan/tilt parts, three camera mounts.',[
        'Support each arm while fastening its mount to the top structure in the vendor orientation. Skip rebuilding an already assembled arm; check for loose joints and cracked prints.',
        'Assemble the head pan/tilt mechanism using the supplied hardware. Mount the head camera and each wrist camera if not already fitted.',
        'Leave the grippers empty. Place the intended plant surface beside the parked cart and check approximate reach without forcing any joint. The planter table is separate from the robot cart.'], 'Both arm bases and the head are secure; cameras have unobstructed views.')
    body+=step(4,'Finish robot cabling before power','Supplied motor harnesses, camera leads, motor-controller USB cables and cable retainers.',[
        'Use the delivered wiring map to connect each motor chain to the correct controller, including head and drive extensions. Label the two controller USB leads so they cannot be confused later.',
        'Keep motor power disconnected while changing motor plugs. Match connector orientation and pin order; do not splice the farm wire into the motor chain.',
        'Retain camera and motor cables at fixed points, with service loops at moving joints. Check that wrist motion cannot pull a plug or stretch a cable.'], 'Robot wiring matches the delivered revision and all plugs are seated with power off.')
    body+='''<p class="small">For unassembled arms, use the <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/main/en/so101">SO101 joint-by-joint instructions</a>. Both XLeRobot arms are followers; a generic leader/follower tutorial does not make one of these a leader.</p></section><section id="computer"><h2>5–8 · Connect the Mac and prove basic motion</h2>'''
    body+=picture('workbench','Mac with powered hub, camera and separate motor power','USB carries control and camera data. Motor power is separate. Probe equipment shown in the broader render is optional for the paper-tray build.')
    body+=step(5,'Connect the powered hub and identify USB devices','RSHTECH RSH-ST07, its adapter and upstream cable, MacBook, robot USB leads.',[
        'Connect the hub’s own supplied power adapter, then its upstream data cable to the Mac’s USB 3-capable port. Keep the Mac on its charger. Do not assume a downstream charging port is the hub’s upstream port.',
        'Plug in one motor controller at a time and record its serial-device name, then connect each camera and identify head, left wrist and right wrist by covering the lens briefly.',
        'Use the connection checklist below for port labels. Nothing on a USB cable alone powers the arm motors.'], 'Two controller identities and three camera identities recorded without guessing indices.')
    body+=step(6,'Install a matching robot software version','Mac, XLeRobot source and compatible LeRobot environment.',[
        'Follow the XLeRobot installation guide and its compatible LeRobot revision; record both versions. Do not mix commands from old tutorials with a newly upgraded environment.',
        'Use the SO101 guide’s port-discovery tool to map the actual Mac serial ports. Configure those paths in the robot settings instead of Linux /dev/ttyACM names.',
        'Select the two-wheel configuration and its expected motor map. Check that the installed code supports the delivered base before commanding it. macOS support for individual serial tools is not proof that every full-robot demo works.'], 'Software versions, Mac ports, camera names and two-wheel profile recorded.')
    body+=step(7,'Power, identify and calibrate one arm at a time','Matched kit motor supply, empty supported arm, reachable power disconnect.',[
        'Confirm adapter input accepts the local mains and output matches the motor labels. Use the supplied wall supplies for commissioning. Apply motor power last; disconnect it before changing motor wiring.',
        'Check the preconfigured motor IDs before changing them. If configuration is required, use the documented one-motor-at-a-time procedure; never rename a whole connected chain blindly.',
        'Calibrate each follower arm with a unique name. Follow the tool’s midpoint and range prompts with torque disabled as directed; support the arm and stay within mechanical limits. Save the resulting calibration files.'], 'Both arms report the expected motors and have separate saved calibrations.')
    body+=step(8,'Test cameras and small movements without water','Assembled robot, all USB devices, empty grippers and empty work area.',[
        'Start a single-joint control example at conservative motion limits. Test direction and stopping on one arm, then the other, then the head. Keep base motion disabled during the first farm setup.',
        'Open the camera streams together at modest supported resolution/frame rate. Label the views and check for dropped frames while moving an arm; lower bandwidth if necessary.',
        'Test the configured stop action with empty grippers and supported arms. Once controls are dependable, rehearse reaching an empty tray and returning to rest.'], 'Small motions, camera coverage and stopping work together without cable pulls or USB resets.')
    body+='''<p class="small">References: <a href="https://xlerobot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/software/getting_started/install.html">XLeRobot installation</a>, <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/main/en/so101">SO101 port setup/calibration</a>, <a href="https://xlerobot.readthedocs.io/en/latest/software/getting_started/SO101.html">single-arm examples</a>. These are setup instructions; no robot software has been installed or tested by this website.</p></section><section id="sensors"><h2>9–11 · Add just one light sensor</h2>'''
    body+=picture('light-tool','One light board mounted on the printed paddle','Use one BH1750, with its sensing face upward. The ESP32 stays on the dry robot body. The gripper holds the paddle only while taking a reading.')
    body+=step(9,'Wire a short light-sensor circuit on the bench','One ELEGOO ESP32, one BH1750, USB data cable, breadboard and jumper wires.',[
        'With USB unplugged, read the labels on both boards. Fit headers only if needed; solder loose headers into their plated holes. Already installed headers require no soldering.',
        'For a verified ESP-WROOM-32 board, our proposed assignment is BH1750 VCC to ESP32 3V3, GND to GND, SDA to GPIO21 and SCL to GPIO22. Match by GPIO label, not physical pin number. Confirm the actual breakout accepts 3.3V and its bus pull-ups do not exceed 3.3V.',
        'Check for shorts, then power the ESP32 only through its USB data cable from the hub. Configure firmware to the same SDA/SCL pins, detect the sensor and log lux over serial. Cover/uncover the sensor and confirm the reading changes.'], 'Light readings reach the Mac and change reliably when shaded.')
    body+=step(10,'Fit the printed paddle and flexible harness','Light paddle prototype, BH1750, flexible wire, retained connector and dry ESP32 enclosure.',[
        'Dry-fit the actual board to the paddle; the prototype is not a guarantee of fit. Secure the board without covering the light-sensitive face or shorting its underside.',
        'Replace the short bench jumpers with a retained four-conductor harness. Keep the ESP32 on the cart and the light board on the paddle. The robot’s supplied servo cables are occupied and are not a spare light-sensor bus.',
        'Provide strain relief at the paddle and cart, service loops at joints, and a parked tool rest. Test the entire intended reach while logging sensor errors. If the longer I²C link fails, shorten/rework it before trusting readings.'], 'Paddle stays secure and the complete moving harness passes a reach test.')
    body+=step(11,'Finish durable connections and repeatable light measurements','Perfboard/terminals if needed, insulation, fasteners and tool rest.',[
        'If using perfboard, solder the headers/terminal pins and required interconnects on the dry bench. Check continuity against the tested circuit. Breadboard contacts are temporary, not a moving-arm connector.',
        'Insulate exposed splices with suitable sleeves or enclosed connectors; heat-shrink is only needed where there is an exposed joint. Fasten the electronics clear of water and keep the USB connector accessible.',
        'Teach a pose at canopy height with the face upward and the robot out of its light path. Stop moving, collect repeated fresh readings and record pose/time. Park the light tool before picking up a bottle. Lux readings do not directly measure plant-useful PPFD.'], 'One reproducible light-reading pose and one reliable tool-rest pose established.')
    body+='''<p class="small">The GPIO assignment above is a project proposal for the recorded ESP-WROOM-32 board, not a verified photo of the delivered ELEGOO pinout. <a href="https://docs.espressif.com/projects/esp-dev-kits/en/latest/esp32/esp32-devkitc/user_guide.html">Espressif reference</a>. The board’s own pin labels and documentation take precedence.</p></section><section id="watering"><h2>12–14 · Add trays and supervised watering</h2>'''
    body+=picture('wick-cutaway','Cutaway of paper wicking from water below the seed surface','The water under the cress tray is a small built-in supply. The robot-held bottle replenishes it. An external tank and pump are not part of this setup.')
    body+=step(12,'Assemble and test both growing kits by hand','Cress trough, holder and four insets; square outer tray and insert; paper and water.',[
        'Follow the combined growing guide for fit, leak and paper-wick tests. Use the square tray’s printed cover when available, or a loose household cover for the trial.',
        'Leave an accessible refill opening and inspection spot. Check that paper wets across the seeded area, not just where it first touches water. The square kit needs an actual capillary connection; simply adding water below an elevated insert does not prove self-watering.',
        'Start on a stable household surface. Add the modular printed table only after its actual joints, stiffness and reach are checked. Use repeatable tray locations so a taught pour target does not move.'], 'Both trays pass hand-operated leak/wetness checks and sit in repeatable positions.')
    body+=step(13,'Teach an empty bottle pickup and return','Small bottle, suitable outlet, bottle rest, robot gripper and empty tray.',[
        'Check grip width and retention with an empty bottle. The selected Nalgene container has a wide mouth: validate a fitted spout or use an existing bottle with a controlled outlet. The rendered spout is not a finished printable design.',
        'Place the bottle in a repeatable rest outside the crop. Teach pickup, approach to the refill opening, upright recovery and return while empty.',
        'Check wrist-camera view, clearance above the tray rim and cable slack through the whole path. A manual spray bottle is for hand use until trigger actuation is proven.'], 'Empty bottle can be picked up, aimed and returned without slipping or colliding.')
    body+=step(14,'Try tiny supervised pours and record results','Small measured water fill, kitchen measuring container, household catch dish and towel.',[
        'First pour into a kitchen measure over a catch dish, with a person supervising. Record starting fill, pose and actual water delivered. Repeat across different fill levels; tilt duration alone does not establish a dose.',
        'After that works, try a small refill of the empty planter. Check for overflow and verify the paper still receives water. Keep the Mac, hub and sensor electronics clear of the wet area.',
        'For the initial crop, a person checks water reserve and paper wetness and authorizes each refill. Cameras record evidence; an obscured view is unknown. Do not replace the removed probe with an unvalidated camera-only autonomous watering rule.'], 'Supervised pours and upright recovery work; a human remains responsible for watering decisions.')
    body+='''<p><a href="growing.html">Open the combined growing and kit instructions</a> · <a href="hardware.html#moisture-decision">Why the moisture probe is optional</a></p></section><section id="later"><h2>15–16 · Add only what the next test needs</h2>'''
    body+=step(15,'Keep optional sensor parts off the first build','SHT31, moisture probe, relay modules and moisture-holder prototypes.',[
        'An optional SHT31 measures air temperature/humidity. If wanted, mount it on the dry body in ventilated air, away from motor/electronics heat; it does not need to ride on the arm. Add it to the verified 3.3V-compatible I²C bus only after checking its labels and address.',
        'Defer the SparkFun moisture probe and its power relay for the paper-tray build. They are retained in the reference diagrams for a future comparison experiment, not required connections.',
        'If later testing the probe, validate repeated contact on sacrificial paper, switched power, dry/damp/wet discrimination and contamination/cleaning before mounting it near a crop. A water-depth solution is a different experiment.'], 'Optional parts explicitly marked installed or deferred; absent probe does not become a fake dry reading.')
    body+=step(16,'Commission battery power and mobility separately','Owned Anker C300 DC, verified conversion cables/adapters, matched robot inputs.',[
        'Keep using wall power until the motor power path is checked. The transcript mentions two USB-C PD-to-12V cables, but their actual availability and electrical suitability remain unverified.',
        'For every powered device, verify negotiated output voltage, polarity, barrel size, continuous/peak current and simultaneous-port behavior. The hub needs its own matched supply path; it cannot be assumed to run from an arbitrary USB-A port.',
        'Secure the battery low on the cart. Test power stability with the full load before untethered driving. Use the two-wheel base profile and a clear area for the first drive test; farm care remains parked.'], 'Battery conversion and base motion separately verified, or explicitly deferred.')
    body+='''</section><section id="connections"><h2>Connection checklist: what plugs into what?</h2><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Destination</th><th>Mount / joining method</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Hub upstream cable</td><td>Mac USB 3-capable port</td><td>Plug-in; hub secured on dry cart.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Motor controller A and B USB</td><td>Two hub data ports</td><td>Kit USB cables; label A/B. Separate matched motor supplies.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Head + two wrist cameras</td><td>Three hub data ports</td><td>Supplied mounts and screws; slack at head/wrist joints.</td></tr>
<tr><td>One ESP32 USB</td><td>Sixth hub data port</td><td>Data-capable cable; dry enclosure on robot body. Seventh port remains spare.</td></tr>
<tr><td>One BH1750</td><td>ESP32 3V3, GND, SDA and SCL</td><td>Printed paddle plus retained four-wire harness; headers may need solder.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Optional SHT31</td><td>Verified compatible ESP32 I²C bus</td><td>Body-mounted; optional air context.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Moisture probe and relay</td><td>Not connected in recommended first build</td><td>Deferred with probe holder and probe-specific harness.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bottle and rest</td><td>Mechanical grip only; no electrical connection</td><td>Measured grip and outlet; water added by a person.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Planters and table</td><td>No electrical connection</td><td>Trays stay on a stable surface; robot reaches them.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Perfboard / breadboard / wire / fasteners</td><td>Support the light circuit and cable retention</td><td>Bench-test first; solder only loose headers, chosen perfboard interconnects or splices.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p>The full robot uses five hub data ports; the light-sensor controller adds one. No added pump, scale, external reservoir, overhead camera or second computer is needed for this supervised configuration.</p><p><strong>Remaining physical fit checks:</strong> delivered kit revision, paddle/board retention, bottle grip/outlet, enclosure/fasteners and moving cable length. These depend on the actual parts; the existing Blender scenes and tool STLs are prototypes, not evidence that those fits have passed.</p></section>'''
    return body
